{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Adopt an existing tracker but enforce a 'Schema-First' contract: no data is logged until a formal schema (JSON Schema or Protobuf) is defined and versioned, treating the schema as the primary artifact and the tool as a dumb store.","counterconditions":["The research workflow is highly exploratory and cannot define a stable schema even for short intervals.","The chosen existing tool does not support metadata attachment or strict validation hooks.","The overhead of maintaining schemas slows down iteration velocity below a critical threshold."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Contract-Driven Development: By making the schema the gatekeeper for logging, the team avoids the 'migration cliff' (unstructured noise) because the structure is imposed at creation time, not migration time. This leverages existing tools' storage capabilities while neutralizing their flexibility risks.","optional_refs":["56a44ca1e5c4aa4266afb7e4e41e35b76747722c563bef7026ea9975839cbe90","7ec797a7bd69c7e6afc1cc3b35d82a719bd3d7323ca34719d432ded12fa496f5"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Build a custom 'micro-tracker' specifically designed as a state machine for the team's unique workflow, ignoring general-purpose features like dashboards or user management, and exporting raw logs to existing tools for visualization only.","counterconditions":["The team lacks the engineering bandwidth to maintain even a minimal custom codebase.","The workflow changes so frequently that the state machine requires constant refactoring.","Visualization needs are complex and require deep integration with the logging logic, not just raw export."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Separation of Concerns via Workflow Encoding: Existing tools encode generic workflows. A custom micro-tracker encodes the specific causal model of the research (as per SRC_004) without the bloat. It solves the 'integration tax' by being the native environment, not a wrapper.","optional_refs":["b1642d037422b51c0be00c872d50beb7dbcd5a47b2733f33a65b653bf158ecb0","f0a7fdb1573a11c5f3064989fd549eaaaafda316476c85270e892ed4058bef0b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Adopt a flexible existing platform but implement a 'Shadow Mode' period where the team runs both a disposable script and the formal tool in parallel for 2 weeks, using the script's output to validate the tool's schema before full migration.","counterconditions":["The team cannot afford the time duplication of running two tracking systems.","The disposable script evolves too rapidly during the shadow period to serve as a stable baseline.","The existing tool's API limits the frequency of writes required for parallel logging."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Empirical Validation of Integration Tax: Instead of guessing the fit, the team measures the 'integration tax' directly. If the effort to map script outputs to the tool exceeds a threshold, they abort adoption. This mitigates the risk of underestimating the migration cliff.","optional_refs":["1a36c8265da994bb07de3d93ed583d987252c54734b21ee0f4f97971471095a3","1e7b9016ae4f1e6b113f16ae1c01e0e90d55e6e3d4fd5e9d2986736c63753538"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Write a custom tracker only if the experiment involves active learning loops where the tracker must act as the controller; otherwise, adopt an existing tool configured to treat all results as provisional.","counterconditions":["The active learning loop can tolerate high latency (minutes/hours), allowing existing tools with webhooks to suffice.","The team's definition of 'active control' is actually just batch processing, which existing tools handle.","The complexity of the custom controller introduces more bugs than the latency it saves."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Control Theory Boundary: As argued in SRC_007, standard trackers are passive. If the research requires the tracker to be an active agent (low-latency feedback), custom builds are mandated. For all other cases, the 'provisional' configuration (SRC_008) suffices.","optional_refs":["f0a7fdb1573a11c5f3064989fd549eaaaafda316476c85270e892ed4058bef0b","9b44d6896584dfd1a67415125aa3cb9d693f74898a29b27e22ca31cf06b0fae1"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Adopt an existing tool but restrict its usage to 'Read-Only' archives; all active experimentation happens in local, version-controlled scripts that generate standardized reports, which are then ingested by the tool.","counterconditions":["The team needs real-time collaboration features that only a central app provides.","The volume of local scripts becomes unmanageable without a central orchestrator.","The existing tool does not support bulk ingestion or API-based archiving."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Push-Based Architecture: Reverses the typical 'app-centric' flow. The source of truth remains the code/scripts (avoiding the migration cliff of moving away from them), and the existing tool serves only as a queryable index. This minimizes integration tax by not trying to make the tool the driver.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","7ec797a7bd69c7e6afc1cc3b35d82a719bd3d7323ca34719d432ded12fa496f5"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}